<section style="padding: 3rem 0 6rem; background:#000; border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Verify Any Certification Before You Trust It</h2>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.92rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">Whatever certificate a factory shows you, check it against the issuing body's own database rather than the PDF they send — global-standard.org for GOTS, the OEKO-TEX certificate checker for OEKO-TEX, wrapcompliance.org for WRAP. A genuine certificate holder won't hesitate to give you the license or certificate number to look up yourself.</p>

    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">When you pull up that record, check these five things specifically, not just whether a result appears at all:</p>
    <ul style="color:#ddd;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:2;padding-left:1.2rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Scope certificate vs. transaction certificate</strong> — a scope certificate covers the factory's general capability; a transaction certificate covers one specific shipment. Ask which one applies to your order.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Expiry date</strong> — most of these run 12 months (some, like WRAP historically, up to 2 years for legacy tiers). An expired certificate is not a current one, whatever the factory says.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">License or certificate number</strong> — this is what you actually search on the issuing body's site, not the factory's name.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Certifying body named on the document</strong> — cross-check it's an accredited body for that standard, not an unfamiliar name attached to a real-looking template.</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Site or scope match</strong> — an audit at one production line or one legal entity doesn't automatically cover a sister facility or a subcontractor used for part of the order.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">How Certifications Get Misused (Greenwashing)</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">A surprising amount of "certified" marketing language doesn't hold up under the checks above. A few patterns worth knowing:</p>
    <ul style="color:#ddd;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:2;padding-left:1.2rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
      <li>"Made with GOTS-certified cotton" is not the same claim as "this finished garment is GOTS-certified" — the fibre can be certified while the cut-and-sew stage isn't.</li>
      <li>A supplier holding a certificate doesn't mean every product they make is covered — check the transaction certificate matches your specific order, not just the factory's general reputation.</li>
      <li>"OEKO-TEX certified fabric" inside a garment doesn't mean the whole finished garment (trims, prints, thread) has been tested — components can slip through if the label only covers the base fabric.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;">For a deeper look at how to spot these patterns and vet a supplier's paperwork properly, see our guides on <a href="/insights/ethical-manufacturing-verification" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">verifying ethical manufacturing claims</a> and <a href="/insights/avoiding-clothing-manufacturer-scams" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">avoiding clothing manufacturer scams</a>.</p>

    <div style="text-align: center; padding: 2.5rem; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; border-top: 0.6px solid #e00; background: #050505; margin-top: 2rem;">
